                         GIT & GITHUB - COMPLETE NOTES
================================================================================

1. BASIC CONCEPTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

GIT:
    - Git is a distributed Version Control System (VCS).
    - It tracks changes in your code on your LOCAL machine.
    - Local Directory = the folder on your computer where you write code.

GITHUB / BITBUCKET:
    - These are REMOTE hosting platforms for Git repositories.
    - They allow teams to store, share, and collaborate on code online.
    - Local Directory  --> pushed to -->  Remote Repository (GitHub/Bitbucket)

WORKFLOW (Local Git Flow):

    Local Directory  --->  Staging Area  --->  Local Repository  --->  Remote Repository
     (working dir)          (git add)           (git commit)            (git push)


================================================================================
2. GIT COMMANDS WITH EXAMPLES
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.1 CLONE A REPOSITORY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Command:
    git clone <repository_url> <directory_name>

Example:
    git clone https://github.com/user/my-project.git my-project
    (Downloads the remote repo into a folder named "my-project")


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.2 CHECK STATUS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Command:
    git status

Example:
    git status
    -> Shows which files are modified, staged, or untracked.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.3 CHECK / VIEW BRANCH
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Command:
    git branch

Example:
    git branch
    -> Lists all local branches and highlights the current branch with a "*".


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.4 STAGING FILES (git add)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Used to move changes from the Working Directory to the Staging Area
before committing.

    git add --all         -> Stage every change (new, modified, deleted) in the project
    git add -A            -> Same as --all, stages everything in the project
    git add .             -> Stage changes only in the current directory (and subfolders)
    git add *             -> Stage new/modified files, but NOT deleted files
    git add <file_name>   -> Stage a specific file
    git add *.txt         -> Stage all files with the ".txt" extension

Examples:
    git add index.html          -> Stages only index.html
    git add *.js                -> Stages all JavaScript files
    git add .                   -> Stages everything changed in current folder


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.5 UNDOING CHANGES (git reset)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    git reset             -> Undo "git add" — moves staged files back to working directory
    git reset HEAD~        -> Undo the last commit, changes go back to working directory
    git reset --hard       -> Discards changes and restores the last committed state
                              (can also bring back recently deleted files)

Examples:
    git reset app.py            -> Unstage app.py (keeps changes in working directory)
    git reset HEAD~1            -> Undo last commit but keep the changes
    git reset --hard HEAD~1     -> Undo last commit AND discard the changes completely


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.6 COMMITTING CHANGES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Command:
    git commit -m "commit message"

Example:
    git commit -m "Added login functionality"

CONFIGURING USER INFO (needed before committing):

    Global config (applies to ALL repositories on your machine):
        git config --global user.email "your@gmail.com"
        git config --global user.name "git username"

    Local config (applies only to the CURRENT repository):
        git config --local user.email "your@gmail.com"
        git config --local user.name "git username"

Example:
    git config --global user.email "john@gmail.com"
    git config --global user.name "John Doe"


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.7 REMOVING FILES (git rm)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    git rm <file_name>          -> Deletes the file AND stages the deletion
    git rm -f <file_name>       -> Force delete the file completely
    git rm --cached <file_name> -> Removes file only from staging (keeps it on disk)
    git rm -r <folder_name>     -> Deletes a folder and its contents (recursively), no staging needed

Examples:
    git rm old_file.txt             -> Deletes old_file.txt and stages the removal
    git rm --cached secrets.env     -> Stops tracking secrets.env but keeps it locally
    git rm -r old_folder/           -> Deletes entire folder


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.8 VIEWING COMMIT HISTORY (git log)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    git log             -> Lists all commits with full details (author, date, message, hash)
    git log --oneline   -> Lists commits in a compact, single-line-per-commit format

Example:
    git log --oneline
    Output:
        a1b2c3d Added login functionality
        e4f5g6h Fixed navbar bug
        i7j8k9l Initial commit


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.9 BRANCHING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    git branch                    -> List all branches
    git branch <new_branch_name>  -> Create a new branch
    git checkout <branch_name>    -> Switch to that branch

Examples:
    git branch feature-login         -> Creates a branch named "feature-login"
    git checkout feature-login       -> Switches to the "feature-login" branch

    (Shortcut to create + switch in one command)
    git checkout -b feature-login


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.10 MERGING BRANCHES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
While on the "dev" branch, to bring "main" branch changes into "dev":
    git checkout dev
    git merge main -m "merging main branch to dev"

While on the "main" branch, to bring "dev" branch changes into "main":
    git checkout main
    git merge dev_branch -m "merge dev to main"

Example:
    git checkout main
    git merge dev -m "merge dev to main"
    -> Combines the "dev" branch history into "main"


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.11 CHECKOUT A SPECIFIC COMMIT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Command:
    git checkout <commit_id>

Example:
    git checkout a1b2c3d
    -> Moves your working directory to the state of that specific commit
       (this puts you in a "detached HEAD" state)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.12 COMPARING COMMITS (git diff)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Command:
    git diff <commit_id_1> <commit_id_2>

Example:
    git diff a1b2c3d e4f5g6h
    -> Shows line-by-line differences between the two commits


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.13 PUSHING TO REMOTE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    git push                 -> Sends local committed changes to the remote repository
    git push origin main     -> Pushes local "main" branch to the "origin" remote

Example:
    git push origin main
    -> Uploads your local commits on "main" to GitHub/Bitbucket


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.14 FETCHING FROM REMOTE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Command:
    git fetch

Example:
    git fetch origin
    -> Downloads new commits/branches from remote WITHOUT merging them into
       your working files. You review changes before merging.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.15 PULLING FROM REMOTE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Command:
    git pull

Note:
    git pull = git fetch + git merge
    (fetches remote changes AND immediately merges them into your current branch)

Example:
    git pull origin main
    -> Updates your local "main" branch with the latest changes from remote


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.16 RESTORING FILES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    git restore <file_name>        -> Restores file to its last committed state
    git restore --staged <file>    -> Unstages a file (keeps changes in working dir)
    git restore --staged .         -> Unstages all files

Examples:
    git restore app.py                  -> Discards uncommitted changes in app.py
    git restore --staged app.py         -> Removes app.py from staging area
    git restore --staged .              -> Unstages everything


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.17 STASHING CHANGES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Stashing temporarily saves uncommitted changes so you can switch branches cleanly.

    git stash                 -> Save current uncommitted changes to a stash
    git stash pop              -> Restore latest stashed changes AND remove it from stash list
    git stash apply             -> Restore latest stashed changes but KEEP it in stash list
    git stash list             -> Show list of all stashes
    git stash pop stash@{0}    -> Restore & remove a specific stash by index
    git stash apply stash@{0}  -> Restore a specific stash but keep it in the list
    git stash drop              -> Delete a stash entry from the list

Example workflow:
    git stash                       -> Save your current changes
    git checkout other-branch       -> Switch branches safely
    git checkout original-branch    -> Come back
    git stash pop                   -> Restore your saved changes


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.18 REVERTING A COMMIT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Command:
    git revert <commit_id>

Example:
    git revert a1b2c3d
    -> Creates a NEW commit that undoes the changes made by commit "a1b2c3d"
       (safe way to undo, since it doesn't rewrite history)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.19 REBASING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Command:
    git rebase <branch_name>

Example:
    git checkout feature-branch
    git rebase main
    -> Moves/replays "feature-branch" commits on top of the latest "main" branch,
       creating a cleaner, linear commit history


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.20 DELETING A BRANCH
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Delete a LOCAL branch:
    git branch -d <branch_name>     -> Safe delete (only if branch is already merged)
    git branch -D <branch_name>     -> Force delete (removes even if unmerged) [-D = capital]

Delete a REMOTE branch:
    git push origin --delete <branch_name>

Examples:
    git branch -d feature-login                  -> Deletes local branch "feature-login" (safe)
    git branch -D feature-login                  -> Force deletes local branch "feature-login"
    git push origin --delete feature-login       -> Deletes "feature-login" from remote (GitHub)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2.21 INITIALIZING A NEW LOCAL REPO & CONNECTING TO A REMOTE (FROM SCRATCH)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Use this workflow when you're starting a brand-new project locally and want to
turn it into a Git repo, then link it to an empty remote repo you created on
GitHub/Bitbucket.

    git init                       -> Initializes a new, empty local Git repository
                                       (creates a hidden ".git" folder in current directory)
    git status                     -> Check current status (shows untracked files)
    git remote add origin <url>    -> Links your local repo to a remote repo, naming it "origin"
    git branch                     -> List branches (confirms which branch you're on)
    git add .                      -> Stage all files in the current directory
    git commit -m "message"        -> Commit the staged files with a message
    git push origin master         -> Push your local "master" branch to the "origin" remote

Example (step-by-step):
    git init
    git status
    git remote add origin <url>
    git branch
    git add .
    git commit -m "Add practice repo"
    git push origin master

Notes:
    - <url> is the remote repository's URL, e.g.
      https://github.com/username/repo-name.git
    - "origin" is just the conventional name for the remote; you could name it
      anything, but "origin" is the standard.
    - Newer versions of Git may default the initial branch to "main" instead of
      "master" — check with "git branch" before pushing, and push to whichever
      branch name actually exists locally (e.g. "git push origin main").
    - If the remote already has commits (e.g. a README created on GitHub), the
      push may be rejected. In that case, either pull first
      ("git pull origin master --allow-unrelated-histories") or push with
      "-u" to set upstream tracking: "git push -u origin master".
    - To check which remotes are already configured: "git remote -v"
    - To remove a remote: "git remote remove origin"


